summary_en.technical_approach:
CLAS replaces the fixed LAS coefficient with a context-dependent linear gate. Standard LAS modifies an activation as h~_{l,t} = h_{l,t} + alpha d_l, where d_l is a unit steering direction extracted from a probe. CLAS instead uses h~_{l,t} = h_{l,t} + (c_l · [h_{l,t}; 1]) d_l. The steering direction d_l remains fixed, while a learned sensing vector c_l reads the current hidden state and outputs a token- and layer-specific steering strength. The appended constant 1 acts as a bias term.

The method has two stages. First, concept probes are trained and per-layer steering directions are extracted, as in prior probe-based steering work. Second, those directions are frozen and only the sensing vectors are learned using standard next-token prediction on a small prompt-completion dataset. This design makes several deliberate trade-offs:
1. interpretability is retained because the semantic direction still comes from the probe;
2. the learnable part is restricted to a linear gate, which keeps parameter count and sample complexity low;
3. unlike LoRA or ReFT, CLAS does not learn a general low-rank residual patch, but a mechanism that decides how strongly to apply an already identified concept direction.

A notable part of the paper is the comparison with ReFT and LoRA not only as steering baselines but also as sources of interpretable directions. The authors test whether the low-rank directions learned by those methods can serve as concept monitors. They can to some extent, but probe-derived directions are consistently more linearly readable, which supports the CLAS decomposition into direction plus gate.

summary_en.experiments:
The evaluation spans 11 steering tasks and 4 instruction-tuned model families. Tasks include step-by-step reasoning, multilingual translation, and TL;DR-style summarization, under a low-data setup where the target behavior is induced from limited completion data rather than explicit instructions. The main empirical findings are consistent across tasks:
1. CLAS outperforms standard LAS on nearly every model-task pair;
2. under limited labeled data, CLAS matches or exceeds ReFT and LoRA;
3. tuning is substantially cheaper than methods that train low-rank adapters, since CLAS only learns one sensing vector per layer;
4. in 11 concept-monitoring tasks, probe-derived directions used by CLAS are more interpretable than the directions implicit in ReFT or LoRA.

The paper also includes interference experiments, which are important: after steering toward one behavior, the authors measure how much other behaviors degrade. CLAS shows more controlled interference than fixed-strength LAS, suggesting that contextual gating reduces the tendency to globally distort the model distribution. The full paper emphasizes robustness trends across tasks more than a single headline metric.

summary_en.reading_guide:
This is a skim paper, but the method section is worth careful attention. A good reading order is:
1. read the introduction to understand that the contribution is dynamic steering strength, not a new steering direction;
2. read the CLAS formula and training setup in the method section, especially why d_l is frozen and only c_l is learned;
3. inspect the main steering results across the 11 tasks;
4. if interpretability matters to you, read the concept-monitoring section closely, since that is where the paper says something beyond simple task performance.

About 30 to 45 minutes should be enough for the main ideas. The most interesting follow-up questions are whether linear gating is sufficient, how stable the probe directions are across layers and tasks, and whether this style of context-sensitive steering could be moved from inference-time control toward pretraining-time representation shaping.
